course: dictionary
lesson: 4301
title: Grind: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. Grind can mean crushing something into powder, rubbing something sharp, rubbing your teeth together, or hard, boring work. The context shows you which meaning is right.
explain: «A miller grinds flour» means crushes grain into powder, because flour is powder made from wheat. «A muscly part of the stomach called the gizzard grinds their food» means breaks food into tiny pieces. In both, grind is about crushing.
explain: Look at the words around grind. «Grinding your teeth at night can harm them» is about rubbing teeth together hard. «Back to the grind» is about hard, boring work. «Grind to a halt» means slowly stop, like a machine that gets slower and slower.
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence: «The stomach crushes the food» still makes sense, so crush is right. If your meaning does not fit, try another one.
fact: «Grind» means to crush something into tiny pieces or powder.
fact: «Grind» can also mean to make something sharp or smooth by rubbing it on a hard surface.
fact: «Grind your teeth» means to rub your teeth together hard and noisily.
fact: «The daily grind» is a noun for hard, boring work that goes on and on.
fact: «Grind to a halt» means to slowly stop. The forms are grind, grinds, ground and grinding: «mills ground grain into flour» uses the past tense.
exercise: «A miller grinds flour.» What does grinds mean here? | Runs very fast | Sings loudly while working | Dresses in warm clothes | *Crushes into tiny pieces or powder
exercise: «Back to the grind» means back to what? | A game with friends | A long, warm sleep | *Hard, boring work | A holiday at the beach
exercise: «The machine began to grind to a halt.» What does grind to a halt mean? | Turn into powder | Start very fast | *Slowly stop | Make a loud bang
test: «Grinding your teeth at night can harm them.» What does grinding mean here? | Smiling widely | Brushing gently with a soft brush | Chewing a sweet slowly | *Rubbing your teeth together hard and noisily
test: «Triceratops had a beak and rows of grinding teeth for tough plants.» What were the grinding teeth for? | Sticking to honey | Tearing meat | Catching fish | *Crushing tough plants
exam: Sam says grind only means crushing flour. What is the best reply? | Yes, it only means teeth | *No, it also means rubbing teeth, rubbing things sharp, and hard, boring work | No, it means to sing loudly | Yes, it always means flour
